  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You have NNN sticks, of integral lengths A1,A2,...,ANA_1, A_2, ..., A_NA1​,A2​,...,AN​.\n\nYou can take any stick of length XXX and break it into 222 **positive integer** lengths YYY and ZZZ such that Y+Z=XY + Z = XY+Z=X, i.e. now instead of the stick of length XXX, you obtained one stick of length YYY and another of length ZZZ.\n\nOne such action is called a **break**. Find the maximum number of **breaks** you can perform.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of input contains NNN - the number of sticks.\nThe second line contains NNN integers - A1,A2,...,ANA_1, A_2, ..., A_NA1​,A2​,...,AN​, the lengths of the sticks\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, find the maximum number of **breaks** you can perform.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤1001 \\le N \\le 1001≤N≤100",
      "1≤Ai≤1001 \\le A_i \\le 1001≤Ai​≤100"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n1 1\n1\n3",
        "output": "0\n2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : There are no possible breaks to do because it is not possible to split 111 into 222 positive integer numbers.\nTest Case 2 : We can break the stick of length 333 into sticks of lengths 222 and 111. Further, we then break the stick of length 222 into sticks of lengths 111 and 111. Thus, we performed 222 breaks."
      }
    ]
  },
